---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <h1>Frequently Asked Questions</h1>
  <h2>Does strict liability apply even if I was on the dog owner&rsquo;s property?</h2>
  <p>In most strict liability states, yes &mdash; if you were lawfully on the property (as an invited guest, mail carrier, or with permission). Trespassers have reduced or no protection in most states. Check your state&rsquo;s specific statute for the trespasser exception.</p>
  <h2>Will the dog owner&rsquo;s homeowners insurance cover my claim?</h2>
  <p>Usually. Homeowners and renters insurance typically covers dog bite liability. Policy limits are commonly $100,000&ndash;$300,000. Some policies exclude certain breeds (pit bulls, Rottweilers, German Shepherds). If the owner lacks insurance or their policy excludes the breed, you must sue the owner directly.</p>
  <h2>What if the dog had no history of aggression?</h2>
  <p>In strict liability states, prior history is irrelevant &mdash; the owner is liable regardless. In one-bite rule states, no prior history makes the case harder because you must show the owner knew or should have known of the dog&rsquo;s dangerous propensity.</p>
  <h2>How long do I have to file a claim?</h2>
  <p>The statute of limitations for dog bite claims is typically 2&ndash;3 years in most states, starting from the date of the bite. Some states have shorter deadlines. Children&rsquo;s claims may toll until age 18.</p>
  <h2>What damages can I recover?</h2>
  <p>Medical expenses (past and future), lost wages, pain and suffering, emotional distress, scarring and disfigurement, and in severe cases, loss of enjoyment of life. Plastic surgery and psychological counseling for bite-related trauma are includable.</p>
  <h2>Do I need an attorney for a dog bite claim?</h2>
  <p>For minor injuries with clear liability, you may be able to settle directly with the homeowners insurance adjuster. For injuries requiring medical treatment, surgery, or permanent scarring, an attorney will typically secure significantly higher settlements and is worth the contingency fee.</p>
</BaseLayout>
